The Wind whispered back, "Beloved maker, I fill the world with sound, but your vessels—they must learn to hold it first, then choose to release it."

Iris descended and began her greatest work. She crafted a bell from clay mixed with her own breath, shaped with memories of laughter and sorrow. Into its curve she pressed the rhythm of her heartbeat. For eleven nights she fired it, cooling it with songs she had never dared to sing aloud.

When she finally struck the bell, it rang with a voice unlike any other—clear, resonant, and wholly its own. The sound rippled outward, teaching every hollow thing in the world to sing.

